  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $U$ contains only polynomials of the form $a{x}^{5},$ where $a$ is a real coefficient. However, $f(x)=5{x}^{3}$ is *not* a polynomial of this form. So, $f(x)\\notin U.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $p(x)=a{x}^{5}\\in U,$ where $a$ is a real coefficient, and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\nk\\cdot p(x)=k(a{x}^{5})=(ka){x}^{5}.\n$$\n\nSince $(ka)$ is a real coefficient, we conclude that $(ka){x}^{5}\\in U,$ i.e., $k\\cdot p(x)\\in U.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
